/*
 * The Examples landing page, the two pages it leads to, one for each set of
 * examples in examples/, and the scripts registered for them, with the formatted description shown above each
 * script's source. Doxygen does not read comments in the scripts themselves, so
 * keep each block here in step with that script's header comment, with the
 * lists on its Examples page, with the scripts under Examples in
 * DoxygenLayout.xml (the side menu) and with the Examples table in README.md.
 *
 * The two sets have the same examples: examples/arduino/ (arduino.py, setup()
 * and loop()) and examples/phyduino/ (phyduino.py, plain MicroPython).
 *
 * These pages replace Doxygen's own list (hidden in DoxygenLayout.xml),
 * which can only show file paths such as arduino/01_basics/blink/main.py. For the same
 * reason each script's page title is set by the hidden example-title span at
 * the start of its block, which a script in footer.html moves into the title.
 */

/**
@page example_scripts Examples

Example scripts for the Phyduino Pico. There are two sets with the same
examples: pick the library you want to use.

| Examples | Uses | Laid out as |
|---|---|---|
| @ref examples_arduino "arduino.py examples" | The Arduino-style functions: `from arduino import *`; pinMode(), digitalWrite(), analogRead() (0-1023), analogWrite() (0-255), delay(), millis() | An Arduino sketch: setup() runs once, then loop() runs over and over |
| @ref examples_phyduino "phyduino.py examples" | The Phyduino Pico library: `import phyduino as mcu`; pin(), pwm(), `led`, analog_read() (0-4095), time.sleep() | A plain MicroPython script: the set-up code at the top runs once, then a `while True:` loop runs over and over |

Choose arduino.py if you know Arduino or want code that reads like an
Arduino sketch, and phyduino.py for the usual MicroPython style and the
board's full resolution.

@par Blink, in each set
arduino.py (the main() at the end of each file is left out here):
@code{.py}
from arduino import *

def setup():
    pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT)

def loop():
    digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH)
    delay(500)
    digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, LOW)
    delay(500)
@endcode
phyduino.py:
@code{.py}
import time
import phyduino as mcu

while True:
    mcu.led.toggle()
    time.sleep(0.5)
@endcode
*/

/**
@page examples_arduino arduino.py examples

Example scripts for the Phyduino Pico that use the Arduino-style functions in
`arduino.py` (`from arduino import *`), in `examples/arduino/`. Each script has
the shape of an Arduino sketch: setup() runs once, then loop() runs over and
over, using pinMode(), digitalWrite(), analogRead() (0-1023), analogWrite()
(0-255), delay() and millis(). The main() at the end of each file starts them;
leave it as it is.

The examples are in folders named after the Arduino IDE's example categories,
with board-specific ones in `phyduino_pico/`. Each example is a `main.py` in its
own folder, because MicroPython runs `main.py` each time the board starts. Each
page below explains what the script does, how to wire it and what it prints,
followed by its source. The same examples written with phyduino.py are on
the @ref examples_phyduino "phyduino.py examples" page.

With the Phyduino Pico firmware on the board, run a script from PyCharm, or with
`mpremote run examples/arduino/<folder>/<name>/main.py`. To have it run every time
the board starts, copy its `main.py` to the board:
`mpremote cp examples/arduino/<folder>/<name>/main.py :`, then press RESET.


@par 01_basics
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref arduino/01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py "Bare Minimum" | The empty setup() and loop() to start a program from |
| @ref arduino/01_basics/blink/main.py "Blink" | Blinking the on-board LED |
| @ref arduino/01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py "Analog Read Serial" | All six analogue pins with analogRead(), 0-1023 |
| @ref arduino/01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py "Digital Read Serial" | Printing the state of a pushbutton |
| @ref arduino/01_basics/fade/main.py "Fade" | Fading the on-board LED with analogWrite() |
| @ref arduino/01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py "Read Analog Voltage" | All six analogue pins in millivolts |

@par 02_digital
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref arduino/02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py "Blink Without Delay" | Timing with millis() instead of delay() |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/button/main.py "Button" | Lighting the LED while a pushbutton is pressed |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/debounce/main.py "Debounce" | Ignoring contact bounce so each press counts once |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py "Digital Input Pullup" | A pushbutton with the pin's own pull-up resistor |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py "State Change Detection" | Counting presses by detecting the edge |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/tone_melody/main.py "Tone Melody" | Playing a tune on a piezo buzzer |
| @ref arduino/02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py "Tone Pitch Follower" | A pitch that follows an analogue input |

@par 03_analog
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref arduino/03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py "Analog In Out Serial" | Setting LED brightness from a potentiometer |
| @ref arduino/03_analog/calibration/main.py "Calibration" | Learning a sensor's range at start-up |
| @ref arduino/03_analog/smoothing/main.py "Smoothing" | A running average of the last 10 readings |

@par 04_communication
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref arduino/04_communication/ascii_table/main.py "ASCII Table" | Characters with their decimal, hex, octal and binary values |
| @ref arduino/04_communication/graph/main.py "Graph" | Sending readings to a serial plotter |
| @ref arduino/04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py "I2C Scanner" | Finding the devices on the I2C bus |
| @ref arduino/04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py "Physical Pixel" | Switching the LED from the computer |
| @ref arduino/04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py "UART Loopback" | Sending and receiving on UART0 |

@par phyduino_pico
Features of this board that the Arduino examples have no match for.
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging" | How the averaging setting steadies a reading |
| @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py "Analog Differential" | The voltage between two pins |
| @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py "VBUS Monitor" | The USB-C supply voltage and the CPU temperature |
| @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power" | Asking a USB-PD charger for a higher voltage |
*/

/**
@page examples_phyduino phyduino.py examples

Example scripts for the Phyduino Pico that use `phyduino.py`
(`import phyduino as mcu`), in `examples/phyduino/`, written the usual
MicroPython way: the set-up code at the top of the file runs once, then a
`while True:` loop runs over and over. Analogue readings are 12-bit (0-4095)
and PWM duty is 0-65535.

The examples are in folders named after the Arduino IDE's example categories,
with board-specific ones in `phyduino_pico/`. Each example is a `main.py` in its
own folder, because MicroPython runs `main.py` each time the board starts. Each
page below explains what the script does, how to wire it and what it prints,
followed by its source. The same examples written with arduino.py are on
the @ref examples_arduino "arduino.py examples" page.

With the Phyduino Pico firmware on the board, run a script from PyCharm, or with
`mpremote run examples/phyduino/<folder>/<name>/main.py`. To have it run every time
the board starts, copy its `main.py` to the board:
`mpremote cp examples/phyduino/<folder>/<name>/main.py :`, then press RESET.


@par 01_basics
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py "Bare Minimum" | The empty program to start from |
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/blink/main.py "Blink" | Blinking the on-board LED |
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py "Analog Read Serial" | All six analogue pins as 12-bit codes |
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py "Digital Read Serial" | Printing the state of a pushbutton |
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/fade/main.py "Fade" | Fading the on-board LED with PWM |
| @ref phyduino/01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py "Read Analog Voltage" | All six analogue pins in millivolts |

@par 02_digital
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py "Blink Without Delay" | Timing with time.ticks_ms() instead of time.sleep() |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/button/main.py "Button" | Lighting the LED while a pushbutton is pressed |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/debounce/main.py "Debounce" | Ignoring contact bounce so each press counts once |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py "Digital Input Pullup" | A pushbutton with the pin's own pull-up resistor |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py "State Change Detection" | Counting presses by detecting the edge |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/tone_melody/main.py "Tone Melody" | Playing a tune on a piezo buzzer |
| @ref phyduino/02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py "Tone Pitch Follower" | A pitch that follows an analogue input |

@par 03_analog
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino/03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py "Analog In Out Serial" | Setting LED brightness from a potentiometer |
| @ref phyduino/03_analog/calibration/main.py "Calibration" | Learning a sensor's range at start-up |
| @ref phyduino/03_analog/smoothing/main.py "Smoothing" | A running average of the last 10 readings |

@par 04_communication
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino/04_communication/ascii_table/main.py "ASCII Table" | Characters with their decimal, hex, octal and binary values |
| @ref phyduino/04_communication/graph/main.py "Graph" | Sending readings to a serial plotter |
| @ref phyduino/04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py "I2C Scanner" | Finding the devices on the I2C bus |
| @ref phyduino/04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py "Physical Pixel" | Switching the LED from the computer |
| @ref phyduino/04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py "UART Loopback" | Sending and receiving on UART0 |

@par phyduino_pico
Features of this board that the Arduino examples have no match for.
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging" | How the averaging setting steadies a reading |
| @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py "Analog Differential" | The voltage between two pins |
| @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py "VBUS Monitor" | The USB-C supply voltage and the CPU temperature |
| @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power" | Asking a USB-PD charger for a higher voltage |
*/

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Bare Minimum example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * The smallest program: set-up code goes in setup(), which runs once, and the
 * main code goes in loop(), which runs over and over. Copy this file to start
 * a new program.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/blink/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board green LED once a second.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pinMode(), digitalWrite(), delay()
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Read Serial example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads all six analogue pins with analogRead() (0-1023), twice a second.
 *
 * A0-A2: 0 = 0 V, 1023 = 3.3 V (RP2040 ADC). A3-A5: 0 = 0 V, 1023 = 2.048 V
 * (on-board MAX11635 ADC).
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND and 3.3 V) to any pin to see it
 * change; keep A3-A5 at or below 2.048 V. Unconnected pins drift.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A0= 512  A1=   0  A2=   0  A3= 512  A4=   0  A5=   0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Read Serial example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the state of a pushbutton on D2, 1 when pressed and 0 when not, ten
 * times a second.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/fade/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Fade example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Fades the on-board LED up and down with PWM, about once every three
 * seconds. analogWrite() takes 0 (off) to 255 (fully on); the brightness goes
 * up in 51 steps of 5, 30 ms apart, then back down.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * analogWrite(), delay()
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Read Analog Voltage example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the voltage on every analogue pin, in millivolts, twice a second.
 *
 * Each analogRead() is scaled with its own pin's full scale (3.3 V for A0-A2,
 * 2.048 V for A3-A5), so the numbers can be compared directly.
 *
 * @par Output
 * A0 to A5 in order:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * 1650.0 0.0 0.0 1024.0 0.0 0.0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink Without Delay example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board LED once a second without delay(), so loop() is free
 * to do other work between changes. Each pass checks how long it has been since
 * the last change with millis(), which never wraps round, so plain subtraction
 * works.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/button/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Button example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lights the on-board LED while a pushbutton on D2 is pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/debounce/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Debounce example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Toggles the on-board LED each time a pushbutton on D2 is pressed, ignoring
 * the contact bounce that makes one press look like several. A new reading
 * only counts once it has stayed the same for `DEBOUNCE_MS`.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Input Pullup example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a pushbutton on D2 using the pin's pull-up resistor, so no other
 * parts are needed, prints its state and lights the on-board LED while it is
 * pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and GND. The pull-up holds D2 at 1 while the button
 * is open, so the logic is inverted: pressed reads 0.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>State Change Detection example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Counts presses of a pushbutton on D2 and lights the on-board LED on every
 * fourth press. It acts on the change from released to pressed (an edge), not
 * on the button being held down.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * on
 * number of button pushes: 1
 * off
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/tone_melody/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Melody example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a short tune on a piezo buzzer on D8, then waits two seconds and
 * plays it again. tone() returns straight away, so delay() waits while the
 * note plays.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND (or a small speaker in series with a
 * 100 Ohm resistor).
 *
 * @par Uses
 * tone(), noTone(), delay()
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Pitch Follower example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a pitch on a piezo buzzer on D8 that follows the voltage on A0, from
 * 120 Hz at 0 V to 1500 Hz at 3.3 V, and prints the reading and the pitch.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND, and a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND
 * and 3.3 V) or a light sensor divider on A0.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * analogRead(), map(), tone()
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog In Out Serial example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a potentiometer on A0 with analogRead(), sets the on-board LED's
 * brightness from it with analogWrite(), and prints both numbers.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper on A0, with its ends on GND and 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sensor = 512   output = 127
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/03_analog/calibration/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Calibration example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Learns the range of a sensor on A0 during the first five seconds, then sets
 * the on-board LED's brightness from where each reading falls in that range.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A sensor, e.g. a light-dependent resistor divider, on A0. While the LED is
 * on at the start, give the sensor its lowest and highest values, e.g. cover
 * it and then shine a light on it.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/03_analog/smoothing/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Smoothing example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints a running average of the last 10 readings of A0, which steadies a
 * noisy signal. Each new reading replaces the oldest one. For averaging done
 * by the board itself, see @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging".
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/04_communication/ascii_table/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>ASCII Table example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the printable ASCII characters, 33 ("!") to 126 ("~"), each with its
 * value in decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary, once, in setup().
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * ASCII Table ~ Character Map
 * !, dec: 33, hex: 21, oct: 41, bin: 100001
 * ", dec: 34, hex: 22, oct: 42, bin: 100010
 * ...
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/04_communication/graph/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Graph example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the reading on A0, one number per line, 50 times a second, for a
 * serial plotter to draw as a graph. Close PyCharm's REPL or mpremote first so
 * the port is free.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>I2C Scanner example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lists the addresses of the I2C devices on SDA (D18) and SCL (D19), every
 * 5 seconds, so a module can be plugged in while it runs.
 *
 * Arduino's Wire has no match here, so this uses `mcu.i2c()` from phyduino.py.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * The board has no pull-up resistors on these pins. Most sensor modules
 * include them; otherwise fit 4.7 kOhm resistors from SDA and SCL to 3.3 V.
 * The on-board USB-PD controller is on a separate bus and does not appear.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Found: 0x3C, 0x76
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Physical Pixel example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Turns the on-board LED on when it receives "H" and off when it receives "L"
 * from the computer. Type the letters in the REPL terminal while the script
 * runs: in PyCharm, run it with Execute File in REPL and type in the REPL tab.
 * `mpremote run` doesn't pass typed letters on, so copy it to the board as
 * `main.py`, press RESET, then type in `mpremote repl`. `sys.stdin.read(1)`
 * waits for the next character, so loop() pauses until one arrives.
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>UART Loopback example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Sends a numbered message out of UART0 and reads it back, twice a second.
 *
 * Arduino's Serial1 has no match here, so this uses `mcu.uart()` from phyduino.py.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a jumper wire from D1 (TX) to D0 (RX) first; without it, nothing
 * comes back.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sent 'hello 0', received 'hello 0'
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Averaging example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Shows how averaging steadies a reading. For each setting, takes 100
 * readings of A0 and A3 and prints the lowest, the highest and the spread
 * between them (in ADC steps), then waits 5 seconds and starts again.
 *
 * Arduino has no averaging setting, so this uses `mcu.analog_averaging()` from
 * phyduino.py, which applies to every analogue pin: A3-A5 average in the
 * external ADC itself, A0-A2 take the samples one after another.
 * analogReadResolution(12) makes analogRead() give the ADCs' own 0-4095 steps.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a steady voltage, e.g. a potentiometer wiper, to A0 (0-3.3 V) and
 * A3 (0-2.048 V), or leave them as they are.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * averaging  1  A0  lowest 2040  highest 2056  spread  16
 * averaging  1  A3  lowest 2046  highest 2050  spread   4
 * ...
 * averaging 32  A0  lowest 2047  highest 2049  spread   2
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Differential example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Measures the voltage between two analogue pins, e.g. across a resistor or a
 * sensor bridge, twice a second.
 *
 * Arduino has no differential reading, so this uses the phyduino.py functions.
 *
 * - **A3 - A4**: true differential. The on-board ADC samples both pins at the
 *   same instant, so noise common to both cancels. Range ±1024 mV in 0.5 mV
 *   steps (a larger difference is measured as two separate readings).
 * - **A0 - A1**: computed from readings taken one after the other. Fine for
 *   slow signals, but noise on both pins does not cancel.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Keep every pin between 0 V and its full scale (A0-A2: 3.3 V, A3-A5: 2.048 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A3 - A4:   100.0 mV    A0 - A1:    99.1 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>VBUS Monitor example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the USB-C supply voltage (VBUS) and the RP2040's temperature once a
 * second. VBUS is about 5 V from a computer; a USB-PD charger can raise it
 * (see @ref arduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power"). VBUS is also on the header's VIN pin.
 *
 * Arduino has no match for these, so this uses the phyduino.py functions.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * VBUS 5.02 V    CPU 24.3 C
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example arduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>USB-PD Power example script (arduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Asks a USB-PD charger for up to `MAX_VOLTS`, then prints the board's
 * profiles, the contract and the measured VBUS once a second.
 *
 * Arduino has no match for this, so this uses `mcu.usb_pd()` and `mcu.uart()` from phyduino.py.
 *
 * A charger takes the only USB-C port, so there is no USB connection to the
 * computer while it is plugged in. Copy this `main.py` to the board so it runs
 * by itself, and watch the output on UART0 (TX on D1, 115200 baud)
 * with a USB-serial adapter. The green POK2 LED lights for the second
 * profile (9 V), POK3 for the third (the 12, 15 or 20 V of `MAX_VOLTS`).
 *
 * @warning VBUS also feeds the VIN pin, so VIN follows the contract:
 * disconnect anything on VIN that can't take the voltage you ask for.
 *
 * The request lasts until the board loses power.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Profiles: 5 V 2 A, 9 V 1 A
 * Contract: offer 2, 1.00 A    VBUS 9.01 V
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Bare Minimum example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * The smallest program: set-up code goes at the top and runs once, and the
 * main code goes inside `while True:`, which runs over and over. Copy this file
 * to start a new program.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/blink/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board green LED once a second.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * `led`: Led.toggle()
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Read Serial example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads all six analogue pins as 12-bit codes (0-4095), twice a second.
 *
 * A0-A2: 0 = 0 V, 4095 = 3.3 V (RP2040 ADC). A3-A5: 0 = 0 V, 4095 = 2.048 V
 * (on-board MAX11635 ADC).
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND and 3.3 V) to any pin to see it
 * change; keep A3-A5 at or below 2.048 V. Unconnected pins drift.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A0=2048  A1=   3  A2=   0  A3=2048  A4=   0  A5=   0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Read Serial example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the state of a pushbutton on D2, 1 when pressed and 0 when not, ten
 * times a second.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/fade/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Fade example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Fades the on-board LED up and down with PWM, about once every three
 * seconds. The duty cycle goes from 0 (off) to 65535 (fully on) in 51 steps,
 * 30 ms apart, then back down.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm()
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Read Analog Voltage example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the voltage on every analogue pin, in millivolts, twice a second.
 *
 * The library scales each pin with its own full scale (3.3 V for A0-A2,
 * 2.048 V for A3-A5), so the numbers can be compared directly.
 *
 * @par Output
 * A0 to A5 in order:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * 1650.0 2.4 0.0 1024.0 0.0 0.0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink Without Delay example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board LED once a second without time.sleep(), so the loop
 * is free to do other work between changes. Each pass checks how long it has
 * been since the last change with `time.ticks_ms()` and `time.ticks_diff()`.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/button/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Button example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lights the on-board LED while a pushbutton on D2 is pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/debounce/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Debounce example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Toggles the on-board LED each time a pushbutton on D2 is pressed, ignoring
 * the contact bounce that makes one press look like several. A new reading
 * only counts once it has stayed the same for `DEBOUNCE_MS`.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Input Pullup example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a pushbutton on D2 using the pin's pull-up resistor, so no other
 * parts are needed, prints its state and lights the on-board LED while it is
 * pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and GND. The pull-up holds D2 at 1 while the button
 * is open, so the logic is inverted: pressed reads 0.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>State Change Detection example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Counts presses of a pushbutton on D2 and lights the on-board LED on every
 * fourth press. It acts on the change from released to pressed (an edge), not
 * on the button being held down.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * on
 * number of button pushes: 1
 * off
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/tone_melody/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Melody example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a short tune on a piezo buzzer on D8, then waits two seconds and
 * plays it again. MicroPython has no `tone()`: a PWM output at 50 % duty
 * makes the note, and a duty of 0 is silence.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND (or a small speaker in series with a
 * 100 Ohm resistor).
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm()
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Pitch Follower example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a pitch on a piezo buzzer on D8 that follows the voltage on A0, from
 * 120 Hz at 0 V to 1500 Hz at 3.3 V, and prints the reading and the pitch.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND, and a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND
 * and 3.3 V) or a light sensor divider on A0.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm(), analog_read()
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog In Out Serial example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a potentiometer on A0, sets the on-board LED's brightness from it
 * with PWM, and prints both numbers.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper on A0, with its ends on GND and 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sensor = 2048   output = 32775
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/03_analog/calibration/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Calibration example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Learns the range of a sensor on A0 during the first five seconds, then sets
 * the on-board LED's brightness from where each reading falls in that range.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A sensor, e.g. a light-dependent resistor divider, on A0. While the LED is
 * on at the start, give the sensor its lowest and highest values, e.g. cover
 * it and then shine a light on it.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/03_analog/smoothing/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Smoothing example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints a running average of the last 10 readings of A0, which steadies a
 * noisy signal. Each new reading replaces the oldest one. For averaging done
 * by the board itself, see @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging".
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/04_communication/ascii_table/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>ASCII Table example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the printable ASCII characters, 33 ("!") to 126 ("~"), each with its
 * value in decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary, then stops.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * ASCII Table ~ Character Map
 * !, dec: 33, hex: 21, oct: 41, bin: 100001
 * ", dec: 34, hex: 22, oct: 42, bin: 100010
 * ...
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/04_communication/graph/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Graph example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the reading on A0, one number per line, 50 times a second, for a
 * serial plotter to draw as a graph. Close PyCharm's REPL or mpremote first so
 * the port is free.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>I2C Scanner example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lists the addresses of the I2C devices on SDA (D18) and SCL (D19), every
 * 5 seconds, so a module can be plugged in while it runs.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * The board has no pull-up resistors on these pins. Most sensor modules
 * include them; otherwise fit 4.7 kOhm resistors from SDA and SCL to 3.3 V.
 * The on-board USB-PD controller is on a separate bus and does not appear.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Found: 0x3C, 0x76
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Physical Pixel example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Turns the on-board LED on when it receives "H" and off when it receives "L"
 * from the computer. Type the letters in the REPL terminal while the script
 * runs: in PyCharm, run it with Execute File in REPL and type in the REPL tab.
 * `mpremote run` doesn't pass typed letters on, so copy it to the board as
 * `main.py`, press RESET, then type in `mpremote repl`. `sys.stdin.read(1)`
 * waits for the next character, so the loop pauses until one arrives.
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>UART Loopback example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Sends a numbered message out of UART0 and reads it back, twice a second.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a jumper wire from D1 (TX) to D0 (RX) first; without it, nothing
 * comes back.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sent 'hello 0', received 'hello 0'
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Averaging example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Shows how averaging steadies a reading. For each setting, takes 100
 * readings of A0 and A3 and prints the lowest, the highest and the spread
 * between them (in ADC steps), then waits 5 seconds and starts again.
 *
 * analog_averaging() applies to every analogue pin: A3-A5 average in the
 * external ADC itself, A0-A2 take the samples one after another.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a steady voltage, e.g. a potentiometer wiper, to A0 (0-3.3 V) and
 * A3 (0-2.048 V), or leave them as they are.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * averaging  1  A0  lowest 2040  highest 2056  spread  16
 * averaging  1  A3  lowest 2046  highest 2050  spread   4
 * ...
 * averaging 32  A0  lowest 2047  highest 2049  spread   2
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Differential example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Measures the voltage between two analogue pins, e.g. across a resistor or a
 * sensor bridge, twice a second.
 *
 * - **A3 - A4**: true differential. The on-board ADC samples both pins at the
 *   same instant, so noise common to both cancels. Range ±1024 mV in 0.5 mV
 *   steps (a larger difference is measured as two separate readings).
 * - **A0 - A1**: computed from readings taken one after the other. Fine for
 *   slow signals, but noise on both pins does not cancel.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Keep every pin between 0 V and its full scale (A0-A2: 3.3 V, A3-A5: 2.048 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A3 - A4:   100.0 mV    A0 - A1:    99.1 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>VBUS Monitor example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the USB-C supply voltage (VBUS) and the RP2040's temperature once a
 * second. VBUS is about 5 V from a computer; a USB-PD charger can raise it
 * (see @ref phyduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power"). VBUS is also on the header's VIN pin.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * VBUS 5.02 V    CPU 24.3 C
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino/phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>USB-PD Power example script (phyduino.py)</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Asks a USB-PD charger for up to `MAX_VOLTS`, then prints the board's
 * profiles, the contract and the measured VBUS once a second.
 *
 * A charger takes the only USB-C port, so there is no USB connection to the
 * computer while it is plugged in. Copy this `main.py` to the board so it runs
 * by itself, and watch the output on UART0 (TX on D1, 115200 baud)
 * with a USB-serial adapter. The green POK2 LED lights for the second
 * profile (9 V), POK3 for the third (the 12, 15 or 20 V of `MAX_VOLTS`).
 *
 * @warning VBUS also feeds the VIN pin, so VIN follows the contract:
 * disconnect anything on VIN that can't take the voltage you ask for.
 *
 * The request lasts until the board loses power.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Profiles: 5 V 2 A, 9 V 1 A
 * Contract: offer 2, 1.00 A    VBUS 9.01 V
 * @endcode
 */
